2,0 $ Growing on Humaria hemisphaerica. It is yellowish becoming tannish and powder like $ Hypomyces stephanomatis
2,0 ,n $ Asci 65-100 X 4.5-7 µm . Its ascospores are 9-12 X 2.5-4 µm . Its anamorph is Stephanoma strigosum and is common unlike the rare teleomorph. It is white to whitish, can be cottony. Conidia 10-15.5X3.5-5.5. Aleuriocondia 12-20. NA and Europe
2,0 $ Growing on Leotia lubrica-Yellow Jelly Babies. It is white to whitish at the beginning to buff then turning greenish or blue-green then darker to almost black $ Hypomyces leotiicola
2,0 ,n $ The simple like perithecia are closely spaced and touching. Asci 60-100 X 4-6 µm . Ascospores 7-10 X 2.5-4 µm . The Anamorph is Sepedonium leotiarum. Conidia 12-17 X 2.5- 4.5 µm , Aleurioconidia 17-25 wide. Conidiophores are 40-60 µm in length***ajout***ccs180££  a faire************Fin ccs180

2,cs180$ Growing on insect fungi

2,0 $ Growing on Helvella, It starts off as a white to whitish mold then turning pinkish then brownish $ Hypomyces cervinigenus
2,0 ,n $ Further details have really complicated the issues of what Helvella we have where and what different species of Hypomyces grow on them. Some of these issues will be discussed on the next page.It can cover the entire host mushroom. For a more accurate macroscopic ID look for the pinkish and brownish colored stages. The ascospores 14-24 X 2-5 µm . Asci 100-140 X 2-5 µm . Species infected include Helvella lacunosa, Helvella griseoalba, and Helvella macropus. Its anamorph is Mycogone cervina and is more commonly encountered, its aleurospores are 13-18 and conidia are 10-24 X 3.5-5 µm .
2,0 $ Growing on Helvella lacunosa, often the host is unrecognizable and rotten, all that is sometimes seen is this small tannish brown colored gilled mushroom $ Clitocybe sclerotoidea
2,0 ,n $ The Parasitic Clitocybe old name was= Tricholoma sclerotoideum. Its cap is 3/8 to 1 inches wide. It has a sclerotium that can vary in size but large enough to easily notice. The Clitocybe spores are 7-10 X 3-3.S µm . "
2,0 $ Growing on Lasiosphaeria ovina $ Krieglsteinera lasiosphaeriae
2,0 ,n $ It looks like tiny transparent hairs that have roundish balls on the outer end. It typically grows in quantity in the Lasiosphaeria but is very small and often a hand lens is needed to see them in detail. Typically a number grow on each Lasiosphaeria growing all over it and typically covering multiple hosts.
2,0 $ Growing on Morels (Morchella) $ Hypomyces cervinigenus
2,0 ,n $ It starts off as a white to whitish mold then turning pinkish then brownish. It can cover the entire host mushroom. For a more accurate macroscopic ID make sure the pink or brownish colored stages are present. The ascospores 14-24 X 2-5 µm . Asci 100-140 X 2-5 µm . Its anamorph is Mycogone cervina and is more commonly encountered, its aleurospores are 13-18 and conidia are 10-24 X 3.5-5 µm . See photo on page 24 where this is on Helvella.

2,0 $ Growing on Gyromitra $ Sphaeronaemella helvellae
2,0 ,n $ Only Gyromitra species affected are Gyromitra infula and Gyromitra ambigua. It makes the host look deformed a little, discolored and weakened. The ascispores are 8-10.5 X 3-4.5 µm . Asci have 8 spores. They are 14-26 X 10-16 µm . Conidia 7-15 X 3-4.5 µm .
2,0 $ Growing on Sclerotina sclerotiorum $ Bionectria compactiuscula
2,0 ,n $ It is yellowish orange to orangish colored and roundish ball in shape. It is typically sunken in the top center. It grows in groups.///It is 8 spored and the asci are 50-60 X 6-8 µm . Ascospores 8-12 X2.5- 4 µm . Conidia 4.5-8.5 X 1.5-3 µm . Its anamorph is Clonostachys compactiuscula./// _ Sclerotina sclerotiorum is just one of the things that this Bionectria grows on including many and more often non fungal things such as soil and dead bark. Also near pyrenomycetes./// _ Sclerotina sclerotiorum starts out looking like a whitish colored cottony mold that casuse plant rot. It produces blackish colored seed looking seed looking areas after the plant typically dies.
2,0 $ Growing on some ascomycetes but also on non-fungal things such as dead bark. It is yellowish orange o orangish colored and resemble tiny roundish balls that are often darker colored in the top center $ Bionectria ochroleuca
2,0 ,n $ Asci are 50-58 X 6.5X7.5 µm . Ascospores 9-11 X 2.5-4 µm . Conidia 4.5-6 X 2-3.5 µm .///_: its anamorph Clonostachys rosea can also grow on myxos.
2,0 $ Growing in the spore bearing layer but not on top of Ascocoryne sarcoides $ Syzygospora sorana
2,0 ,n $ The spores are 7.5-10 X 6-8.5 µm .
2,0 $ Growing on the greyish pink to yellowish green colored discomycete Colpoma juniper which resembles a scab cut into a branches bark. The attacker is jelly like and off white and transulent $ Tremella karstenii= Tremella juniperina. 
2,0 ,n $ Spores of the attacker are globe like or almost so and are 4-6 µm .
2,0 $ Growing on Nectria cinnabarina, called the Coral Spot Fungi. Its fruiting body is up to ,3mm. In eastern North America growing on Beech - Fagus grandifolia, and in Europe on Littleleaf Linden - Tilia cordata wide $ Nitschkia parasitans var. mijuskovicii
2,0 ,n $ Nectria cinnabarina anamorph is Tubercularia vulgaris. The Nitschkia asci are 35-65 X 5-10 µm . They have 8 spores. Ascospores are 5-11 X 2-3 µm .
2,0 $ Growing on Nectria coccinea and Nectria coccinea var. faginata. That causes the beech disease on the trees and on Nectria galligena on cankers of Betula - Birch. Looks like large irregular shaped orange colored patches on the bark what are made up of orangish colored somewhat roundish balls that can sometimes be powdery $ Nematogonum ferrugineum= Gonatorrhodiella highlei 
2,0 ,n $ This fungi imperfecti has Conidia 14-18 X 9-1 l µm .
2,0 $ Growing on Pyrenomycetes $ Nitschkia brevispina
2,0 ,n $ Ascospores 9-15X3-4


3,cs31$ Growing on the Earth Tongue both Geoglossum sp. and Trichoglossum sp. Covering the host or at least part of it starting with the head and turning it whitish 
3,0 ,n $ The NA Anamorph is Papulaspora Candida, which is what, is most commonly found and it resembles a circle with petals around it like a flower. In NA the Conidia 10-18 X 3- 5 µm and Papulospores 18-28 µm . Conidiophores 40-1 OO µm in length.

rem$ 4,cs2 $ Growing on polypores $ See A-S Below cs2
rem$ 5,cs3 $ Growing on Stereum $ " See A-F Below

rem$ 6,0 $ Jelly like, growing on Peniophora sp. A crust Fungi $ Tremella mesenterica=Tremella lutescens 
rem$ 6,0 ,n $ -The Witches Butter. It is faint yellowish to yellowish orange colored. Jelly like. Spores 9-18 X 8-12 µm . Typically on hardwood. A favorite host is Peniophora aurantiaca.

7,0 $ Growing on Tylopilus rubrubrunneus $ Hypomyces melanocarpus 
7,0 ,n $  Really deforms the cap and deforms the stalk somewhat. The Ascospores 28-36 X 7.5-10 µm . The anamorph is a Sepedonium.
7,0 $ Growing on Suillus pictus = Suillus Spraguei, it is whitish becoming yellowish or olive brown in color $ Hypomyces completus
7,0 ,n $  Asci 175-210 X 5-7 µm . Ascospores 25-36 X 5-9 µm . It also occasionally grows on other Boletales but this is the most common and easiest host witch to ID it on. It can cover all the outer parts of the host Suillus. Anamorph Sepedonium brunneum, the imperfect, which is a lot more common of the 2. It is white and cotton like and not firm as above then becoming a hot chocolate powder colored brown and it is then powdery. Conidi a 15-24 X 5.5- 8 µm . The aleurioconidia 17-23 and round to roundish.

7,0$ It is white to whitish becoming yellowish or darker. Growing on many types of boletes but often the host Bolete is unidentifiable. A microscope will also be needed to make the final ID$ Hypomyces chrysospermus 
7,0 ,n $  Also reported on Paxillus and Rhizopogon. Grows on Boletaceae. Commonly called Golden Hypomyces it has 3 forms first the whitish and chalk like Verticillium) stage then turning yellowish Anamorph- Sepedonium chrysospermum where it is very powder like, then orange to reddish brown when it finally becomes pimpled. The final stage is very rare. The entire host mushroom eventually gets attacked. Asci 100-160 X 6.5-9 µm . Ascospores 14-22 X 4-6 µm . Really like Xerocomus. The conidia are 7-22 X 3-10 µm . Aleurioconidia 12-22 round to roundish: I was told The Hypomyces 3-4 µm . in CA on xerocomus-type boletes includes four species - two each in the H. microspermus and H.chrysospermus clades. They may be published by UC Riverside people in the next couple years.. Micro : Compare with Hypomyces microspermus. Macroscopic ID is not possible. The spore size must be checked.H. microspermus has spores that are 10-14 X 3-4 _
7,0 $ Also growing on at least several species of boletes. The perithecia pimples are buff to orangish to orangish brown colored or also having reddish tones. Basically the attacker is white to whitish at first turning buff to brown in color with age $ Hypomyces boletiphagus 
7,0 ,n $  Asci 65-95 X 4-5 µm . Ascospores 8-14 X 2.5-4 µm . Hosts include Austroboletus, Gyroporus castaneus, Strobilomyces etc. It is uncommon. KOH makes yellowish to reddish. Its anamorph is Sepedonium sp. which is buff to light brown colored. Conidia 7-16 X 2-5 µm . Aleurioconidia 22-30 µm 
7,0 $ Growing on boletes white to buff or yellow or yellowish brown. The pimple like perithecia, are buff, amber or brown. It can cover all parts of the host $ Hypomyces chlorinigenus 
7,0 ,n $  Asci 70-90 X 3-5 µm . Ascospores 7.5-12 X 2-4 µm ///Hosts can include the anamorph is Sepedonium chlorinum which starts off white and cotton like but becomes yellowish. Conidia 7-14 X 2.5- 6 µm . Aleurioconidia 30-45 X 15-20 µm .
7,0 $ It is white to whitish becoming yellowish. The pimples are yellow to orange or red or brown. The ascospores also turn purple in KOH. A microscope will also be needed to make the final ID $ Hypomyces inicrospermus 
7,0 ,n $  Asci 110-120 X 4-6 µm . Its ascospores are 8-14 X 3-4 µm .///It grows on many types of boletes including Boletus badius and Xerocomus. All parts of the host can become attacked. The perithecia pimples turn purple in KOH. The anamorph is Sepedonium microspermuni It is cottony and white to yellowish colored. Conidia 8-15 X 3-6 µm . Its aleuriocondia are 9-15 µm and round to roundish.Compare with Hypomyces chrysospermus. Macroscopic ID is not possible. The spore size must be checked. Hypomyces chrysospermus has spores that are 14-22 X 4- 6 µm ._: I was told The Hypomyces in CA on xerocomus-type boletes includes four species - two each in the H. microspermus and H. chrysospermus clades. They may be published by UC Riverside people in the next couple years.

8,0 $ Growing on a tooth fungi Steccherinum adustum = Mycorrhaphium adustum. It is typically found when orangish colored and peritecia pimpled looking but can occasionally be whitish then reddish when mature $ Hypomyces aurantius 
8,0,n$  Also grows on Polypores such as Trametes. It can cover the pores or teeth or the entire host fungi. When finding it on Steccherinum I typically see it only covering the teeth and have to turn the mushroom over to spot it. It turns reddish purple to purplish with a drop of KOH. Asci 100-160 X 6-7 µm . Ascospores 20-25 µm x 4-6 µm . The white colored anamorph is Cladobotryum varium. Conidia 10-20 X 6-8 µm .Hypomyces subiculosus looks similar but grows in the southern North America and not northern. Its ascospores differ by being 1416 X 4-6 µm .

9,0 $ Growing on Agaricus. It is whitish colored and makes the Agaricus develop deformed. It is most commonly identified on 9,0 Agaricus bisporus in mushroom farms but can grow on other types of mushrooms. Commonly called the Wet BubbleDisease $ Mycogone perniciosa =Hypomyces perniciosus. 
9,0 ,n $  Aleuriospores 10.5-15 X 2.5-3.5 µm . Others include Coprinus, Pluteus, and Panaeolus.
9,0 $ Suspected to be growing on or affecting Agaricus bisporus mycelium in commercial mushroom farms $ Clitopilus passeckerianus
9,0 ,n $ _ there are a couple Clitopilus that are suspected of doing this. They have pinkish colored spore prints.
9,0 $ Growing on Agaricus bistorus caps causing spotting $ Aphanocladiuin album
9,0 ,n $ Not this is not the only Agaricus cap spot. Microscopy is needed for the correct ID
9,cs4 $ Growing on Amanita $ See A-D Below


9,0 $ Growing on Armillaria or at least attacking it $ Entoloma abortivum 
9,0 ,n $ In the top photo the Aborted Entoloma is in the middle.The Armillaria on the left and Entoloma on the right. The Aborted ones are on the bottom in the bottom photo.
9,0 $ Growing on Clitocybe sp especially Clitocybe nebularis, typically deforming the host some, has gills and a large sack at the base of the stalk $ Volvariella surrecta
9,0 ,n $ Its cap is 1-3 inches wide. The gills are whitish turning pinkish as the spores mature. Its smooth spores 5-7.5 X 3-6.5 µm It has been also been reported on Tricholoma.
9,0 $ Growing on Coprinus comatus $ Psathyrella epimyces
9,0 ,n $ When the Shaggy Mane gets attack it typically is deformed. The Parasitic Psathyrella has spores that are 7-9.5 X 4-5 µm . Its cap is y2 to 2 VA inches wide. It spore print has a lot of black.
9,0 $ Growing on Crepidotus $ Hypomyces tremellicola= Nectriopsis tremellicola.
9,0 ,n $  This make the host turn whitish and its whitish. Its pimples are tannish to orangish brown. Sometimes we can not determine the host it is so deformed Asci 60-75 X 4-5 µm . Ascispores 7-14 X 3-4 µm . The white colored and cotton like anamorph is Verticillium sp. or like. Conidia 5-9 X 2- 3 µm .
 
9,cs5$ Growing on Gymnopus dryophila or Gymnopus butyracea was Collybia appearing like a jellylike mass one the mushrooms $ See A,B,C,D Below
9,0 ,n $ _ of the 3 Confirmed Species, in box below, that occur in North America, they can not be told apart with out a microscope. _ this Genus was called Christansena in many field guides.

9,0 $ Growing on Gymnopus subnudus $ Hypocrea avellanea
9,0 ,n $ It can grow on the cap, gills and stem. It is brownish and has pimples-the perithecia. Asci 55-90 X 3-5 µm . Has 16 Distal part-ascospores 2-4 X 2-3.5 µm , proximal part-ascospores 2.5-5 X 2-3.5 µm . Conidia 3-9 X 1.5-5 µm . At first it has a white colored Verticillium stage
9,cs6 $ Growing on Lactarius $ See A-F BelowIci cs6  *********


9,0 $ Growing on older Lepista nuda = Clitocybe nuda the Blewit $ Harziella capitata
9,0 ,n $ current name is Lepisticola capitata/// A thin whitish soft moldy covering on gills or at least places on the gills. Perhaps others grow on the Blewit but this is the only thing I could find. In the US and France and Germany.
9,0 $ Growing on Leptonia, Entoloma, & Pouzarella $ Hypomyces porphyreus
9,0 ,n $ Can cover the entire host. The peritheica pimples when they develop are reddish brown. Asci 120-190 X 4-5 µm . Ascospores 20-30 X 3.5-5.0 µm . Most commonly reported on Leptonia strigoissima.
9,cs7 $ Growing on Marasmius pallidocephalus, it is jelly like and covers the entire cap of the host deforming it and making it unrecognizable. Stalk not affected. There are 3 species that can not be told apart without a microscope since they all look the same 

9,0 $ Growing on small Marasmioid looking gilled fungi. It can be yellowish in color and grow on both the cap and stalk $ Neobarya agaricicola
9,0 ,n $ Asci 140-350 X 3.5-6 µm . Conidia 6-9 X 3-4.5 µm .
9,10 $ Growing on Mycena appearing as hairs at least covering the cap : Spinellus sp.
9,10 ,n $ This mushroom has the common name of Bad Hair Day Fungus. It typically is encountered on the Bleeding Mycena - Mycena haematopus but also other Mycena species and even some other gilled species. See below for additional microscopic Key. Macroscopically we can not continue. Only 3 Species from North America. See box below for NA.Other Species include Spinellus sphaerosporus spores globe shaped approx. l0 µm Spinellus gigasporus 12-15 X 50-60 µm .

