1,0$  Growing on The Birch Polypore - Piptoporus betulinus where it is typically found but also on Fomitopsis pinicola and others but less commonly encountered on these hosts. Yellowish in color sometimes also having orange or off brown tones in addition, and typically found on the pores $ Hypocrea americana 
1,0,n $    was Hypocrea citrina var. americana this mushrooms name changed when it became its own species. Asci 50-100 X 3.5 6 µm . Its distal part-ascospores are 2.5-4 X 3-4.5 µm , proximal part-ascospores 2.5-4 X 3-4 5 µm and are almost the same length and width.
1,0$ Growing on various polypores turning them reddish, hosts include the Birch Polypore - Piptoporus betulinus, it is a very small .2-.5mm creamy to blackish, cup shaped asco$ Hyphodiscus hymeniophilus
1,0,n $    The ascospores are 5.5-8.5 X 2.5-3.5 µm . Its anamorph is Catenulifera rhodogena. Conidia 2.5-5 X 1.5-2.5 µm .
1,0$  Growing on the underside of older Trametes versicolor and Trichaptum biforme. The 2 forms with look like either small black pimples on the pores or small black hair look at the tubes $ Ascocodinaea polyporicola 
1,0,n $    The loupe will often be needed to see them. It is 8 spored. Ascospores 12.5-16 X 5-6.5 µm . The anamorph stage is the one that looks like hairs but are really the conidiophores. Although the photo below by Noah Siegel is growing on Fomes fomentarius we are not certain what it is but you can see hairs
1,0$  Growing on Trametes versicolor and others but most common on it, buff around the pimples which are orangish, reddish orange, salmon-brown, or peach colored $ Hypomyces polyporinus 
